    "['nhrp entry [parameter1] [parameter2] register', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ip nhrp map 172.16.1.1 10.1.1.10', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the HUAWEI NHRP configuration:\\n\\n##\\nip nhrp map 172.16.1.1 10.1.1.10\\nip nhrp nhs 172.16.1.1 nbma 10.1.1.10\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Cisco implementation requires two commands to achieve the same functionality as Huawei\\'s single command. The first command creates the NHRP mapping, and the second designates the Next Hop Server (NHS) with its NBMA address. The \"register\" functionality is implicit in Cisco when you configure an NHS.', 'trans_template': 'ip nhrp map 172.16.1.1 10.1.1.10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ip nhrp nhs 172.16.1.1 nbma 10.1.1.10', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the HUAWEI NHRP configuration:\\n\\n##\\nip nhrp map 172.16.1.1 10.1.1.10\\nip nhrp nhs 172.16.1.1 nbma 10.1.1.10\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Cisco implementation requires two commands to achieve the same functionality as Huawei\\'s single command. The first command creates the NHRP mapping, and the second designates the Next Hop Server (NHS) with its NBMA address. The \"register\" functionality is implicit in Cisco when you configure an NHS.', 'trans_template': 'ip nhrp nhs 172.16.1.1 nbma 10.1.1.10'}]]": 1,
    "['tunnel protection ipsec profile [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'tunnel protection ipsec profile IPSEC-PROFILE', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ntunnel protection ipsec profile IPSEC-PROFILE\\n##\\n\\nThe equivalent Cisco command would be:\\n\\n##\\ntunnel protection ipsec profile IPSEC-PROFILE shared\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The Cisco equivalent is nearly identical in syntax\\n2. The \"shared\" keyword is typically added in Cisco configurations to indicate the profile can be shared by multiple tunnels\\n3. The IPSEC-PROFILE would need to be separately defined in Cisco\\'s crypto configuration (just like in Huawei)', 'trans_template': 'tunnel protection ipsec profile IPSEC-PROFILE'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'tunnel protection ipsec profile IPSEC-PROFILE shared', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ntunnel protection ipsec profile IPSEC-PROFILE\\n##\\n\\nThe equivalent Cisco command would be:\\n\\n##\\ntunnel protection ipsec profile IPSEC-PROFILE shared\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The Cisco equivalent is nearly identical in syntax\\n2. The \"shared\" keyword is typically added in Cisco configurations to indicate the profile can be shared by multiple tunnels\\n3. The IPSEC-PROFILE would need to be separately defined in Cisco\\'s crypto configuration (just like in Huawei)', 'trans_template': 'tunnel protection ipsec profile IPSEC-PROFILE shared'}]]": 1,
